     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hello,</div><div><br></div><div>Is it possible to use group covariates with the polygon detector? I run my sexes separately, so up until now have not needed a covariate column - but I now need to differentiate two groups within each sex (collared vs. non-collared), but looking at the vignettes, it appears even setting <span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\">&quot;covnames = c(&quot;Covariate&quot;) </span>in my <span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\">read.capthist() </span>call, it gets dropped. <br></div><div><br></div><div>What is the correct way to bringing in individual or group covariates using polygon detectors? I have an additional column in my capthist textfile that is used in<span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\"> read.capthist()</span>.<code></code><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">capsfem </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> read</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">capthist</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;captXY.txt&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;perimeter.txt&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> fmt </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;XY&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> detector </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;polygon&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> covnames </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;Collared&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>capsfemdiscrete </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> discretize</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">capsfem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> spacing </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1500</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> outputdetector </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;proximity&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#800\">#no covariates get passed on to </span><b><span style=\"color:#800\">capsfem</span></b><span style=\"color:#800\"> so </span><b><span style=\"color:#800\">capsfemdiscrete</span></b><span style=\"color:#800\"> is also without covariates</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>The code I attempted to use for <span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\">secr.fit():</span></div><div><code></code><br></div><div><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">m1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> secr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">fit</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">capthist</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;capsfem&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> buffer </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">15000</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> list</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">D</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> g0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\">g</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> trace </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br></div><div>I know this code will not work since the covariates are not kept with my <span style=\"font-family:courier new,monospace\">read.capthist </span>code. <br></div><div><br></div><div>How can I keep individual, group, or h2 covariates with polygon detectors?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks,</div><div>Sam<br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
